WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.020
This episode was made possible by generous
supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.080
Hey Crazies.

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:11.270
There still seems to be some confusion about
how relativity works.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:14.760
Before we do any more videos, let’s
straighten a few things out.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.580
Here are the fundamental rules of relativity.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.040
Rules-ativity?

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:19.800
Nah.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.240
Here are the rules you should know:

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.760
Rule #1: No point of view has preference over
any other point of view.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:26.760
Ever Ever?

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.840
Ever Ever.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.580
That’s what it means for things to be relative.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.300
Does the Moon orbit the Earth or does it orbit
the Sun?

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.740
Depends on your point of view.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.020
Both are correct.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.280
Do the planets orbit the Sun in circles or
do they orbit the Sun in spirals?

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.840
Depends on your point of view.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:42.940
Both are correct.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.800
Does this squirrel accelerate down toward
the ground?

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.740
Or does the ground accelerate up toward the
squirrel?

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.520
It depends on your point of view!

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:51.880
Both are correct!

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:57.100
Even though two observers can and often do
disagree, neither one of them is wrong.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.020
Well, as long as they’re taking measurements
properly.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.400
Which brings me to:

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:07.180
Rule #2: Every observer carries their own
set of coordinates and their own clock.

00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:11.570
The real world has dimensions and it’s filled
with paths and shapes.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.700
The things that we see around us look geometric.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.610
But geometry isn’t a very versatile form
of math on its own, so we invented something

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.360
called a Coordinate System.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.980
A device that turns geometry into algebra.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.960
It’s a way for us to label positions with
numbers and label directions with signs.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.460
We’ve already established that no one’s
point of view gets preference.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.140
So everyone has their own set of coordinates
relative to themselves.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.900
And, because this isn’t just about space,
it’s also about time,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.180
those coordinates include a clock that measures
their personal time.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.940
Some people call that proper time, but there’s
nothing proper about it.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.960
Don’t forget Rule #1: No one is proper!

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.180
Everyone’s time is on equal footing.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.700
It’s a terrible name, so I’m not going
to use it.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:57.840
I do what I want.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.900
The point is, as long as everyone is taking
measurements using their own coordinates and

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.040
clock,
it doesn’t matter if they disagree.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.250
It’s fine.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:10.800
Rule #3: If one observer wants to take measurements
for a different observer,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.180
then they need a coordinate transformation.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:18.120
As the name suggests, it transforms one set
of coordinates into another.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.500
For special relativity, those are called Lorentz
Transformations,

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.660
named after Hendrik Lorentz because he came
up with the ones we use today.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.840
Wow, Einstein gets a lot of credit for other
people’s work.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:29.500
Huh.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.980
For general relativity, the transformation
depends on what the geometry looks like.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.340
Around the Earth, the transformation for time
looks something like this:

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.520
Time passes slower on the surface than it
does farther away in space.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:43.620
That looks like math.

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:44.629
Is that math?!

00:02:44.629 --> 00:02:46.360
I didn’t sign up for math.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.720
Yes, yes, it’s math, but we going to try
to work around it.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.900
Consider two observers: Me and You.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.200
Let’s assume I’m in a space station in
the middle of empty space

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.240
Because that happens to be exactly where I
am.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:04.040
Let’s also assume you are in a spaceship
traveling at, say, 55% the speed of light.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:04.920
Fast Fast!

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.500
Now let’s remember the rules.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.320
One!

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.319
No point-of-view has preference.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:14.180
So neither one of us is special in any way
at least as far as space-time is concerned.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:14.940
Two!

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.980
Every observer carries their own set of coordinates
and their own clock.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.980
That looks like this for the two of us, at
least from my point of view.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:28.079
If I want to get an idea of what’s happening
from your point of view, I need Rule #3.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.040
I need a coordinate transformation.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:33.180
Physically, that transformation would look
like this.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.310
Now you’re stationary and I’m the one
that’s moving.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.820
And, according to Rule #1, both points of
view are correct.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.920
Both of these are equally correct.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:46.120
Whenever you work with relativity, you’ve
got two options:

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:51.240
You can do the hideous math in all its glory,
which works, but isn’t very interesting

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.300
or insightful.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:53.160
Or!

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.740
You can use a space-time diagram to make the
math easier,

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:01.140
which is more profound, but you have to accept
a couple weird visual things.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.440
If you haven’t guessed yet, I’m a fan
of the second option.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.320
To draw one of these space-time diagrams properly,
we need another rule.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:13.120
Rule #4: It will always look to an observer
as though they are stationary in space.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.460
Just to clarify, it doesn’t matter what
you think you’re doing.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:19.180
All that matters is what it looks like you’re
doing.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.160
If you’re in a car that’s driving, you
certainly have a concept that you're moving

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.200
because your experience tells you that
things like buildings can’t move.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.020
But!

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.320
It doesn’t look very different from when
you’re sitting on a couch watching TV.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.640
It’s the things around you that are moving
in both cases.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.040
So what’s that have to do with space-time
diagrams?

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:38.940
Right. Right.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.600
That’s where I was going with this.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.660
Let’s go back to you and me in space.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.460
Here’s your spaceship again traveling at
a steady 55% the speed of light.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:52.140
If we focus on that for a minute and give
it some coordinates, that looks like this.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.300
But that’s just a space diagram.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:59.100
We want a space-time diagram,
so we add a time axis and show the spaceship

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.880
moving through time as well.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.520
This is your ship’s space-time path.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.460
My space station’s path is here.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.480
Notice that my path lines up with the time
axis.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.840
That means these coordinates are mine.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.620
Your time axis lines up with your path.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:19.120
In a space-time diagram, a coordinate 
transformation is just a rotation,

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.300
but there's something a little weird happening here.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:26.220
We’ve observed that space and time share
similar but opposite behavior.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:29.980
When the time axis rotates, the space axis
rotates,

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:31.700
but in the opposite direction.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:35.040
These are my coordinates and these are yours.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.040
Each time axis is perpendicular to each space
axis

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:40.340
including this one.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.720
Yes, these are perpendicular.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.360
Like I said, things look weird in space-time
diagrams, but we can get powerful insights.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.560
Did you notice what happened with the
paths?

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:51.860
They didn’t change.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:55.860
The coordinates changed, but the paths stayed
the same.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:01.920
Rule #5: Space-time paths are invariant under
coordinate transformations.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.980
Observers like you and me might measure different
amounts of space or different amounts of time,

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.980
but we all agree on the space-time path.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:15.060
For example, let’s say you started your
stopwatch here and stopped it here.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:18.240
You would say this much time had passed on
your clock.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.200
Using my own stopwatch, I would say this much
time had passed

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:25.230
and I would say you went this far through
space.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:28.020
We might disagree on the coordinates and the
time,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.520
but we both agree you took this path.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:34.560
We just need a coordinate transformation to
properly compare the measurements

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.210
and if we strictly obey these rules, we see
some even weirder things.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.830
Let’s say this is the back of your spaceship
and this is the front.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:47.340
Now let’s say you have a light at each end
that blink at the same time, like this.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.680
But I will not see them blink at the same
time.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.530
For me, those events happen at different times.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.950
I see the lights blink like this.

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:59.200
So we can disagree on how much time passes,
how far your ship has traveled,

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.770
or even how much space your ship takes up.

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:06.670
For one observer, distance or time could even
be zero, but not both.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:10.360
If one observer says things happened at the
same place and time,

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:13.740
then that’s just one event and everyone
agrees on that.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:17.380
Alright, so we may or may not disagree about
things depending on the situation,

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.920
but there’s always one final rule that we
can count on:

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:26.300
Rule #6: Causality must be maintained, no
matter what.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:31.400
Causality, also known as Cause and Effect,
must hold for all observers.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:36.680
If one event causes another, then we’ll all agree
they happened in a particular order.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.760
The cause will always happen before the effect.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.820
Different amounts of time before, but always
before.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:45.880
This is the rule that limits the speed of
light and, frankly,

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:48.040
the speed of anything that could cause an
event.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:51.980
Even if you think you’ve come up with some
clever scenario, Conway79.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:55.220
Let’s say I’m on my space station observing
two ships.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:08:00.120
I see these ships moving at 55% the speed
of light in opposite directions.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:00.880
Fast Fast!

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:04.360
If we shift to one of their points of view,
you might think they observe

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:08.480
the other ship moving away at
110% the speed of light,

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.220
but that would be incorrect.

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:14.180
Remember Rule #3: We need the coordinate transformation.

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.900
The transformation between those two points
of view

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:20.160
is not what you would expect from your personal
experience.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.760
The transformations for velocity actually
look like this.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.340
eBook, shameless promotion, eBook.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:29.840
So one ship would actually measure the other
ship’s speed to be:

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.780
84.5% the speed of light,
which is still less than the speed of light.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.620
You have to remember all the rules if you
want to work with relativity properly.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:43.060
Rule #1: No point of view has preference over
any other point of view.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:48.400
Rule #2: Every observer carries their own
set of coordinates and their own clock.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:53.120
Rule #3: If one observer wants to take measurements
for a different observer,

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:55.260
then they need a coordinate transformation.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:09:00.560
Rule #4: It will always look to an observer
as though they are stationary in space.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:05.600
Rule #5: Space-time paths are invariant under
coordinate transformations.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:10.240
And Rule #6: Causality must be maintained,
no matter what.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:14.440
Those are the rules that space-time lives
by and we have to live with the consequences.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:15.720
Even if they’re weird.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.640
So, got any questions about relativity?

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:19.120
Please ask in the comments.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:20.900
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.100
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like
to keep up with us.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.800
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.520
The featured comment comes from Jack Beavington
who asked:

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:33.240
Can light be affected by gravity?

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:34.160
Absolutely!

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.040
Gravity is curvature in space-time, so anything
traveling through space-time

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:39.360
will be affected by it.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:40.280
Even light!

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:41.620
Thanks for watching!

